# Golden files are compared byte for byte (docs/architecture.md §11). Git must
# not rewrite their line endings on checkout, or a fresh clone on a machine with
# `core.autocrlf=true` fails the comparison for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the exporter.
crates/ondin-export/tests/goldens/** -text

# Scripts and packaging metadata run or parsed on Linux and macOS (§15 D956):
# LF whatever the checkout's `core.autocrlf`, or a CRLF `install.sh` piped into
# bash fails on its first line, and a CRLF `.desktop` or spec is refused.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.spec text eol=lf
*.desktop text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.in text eol=lf

# Rust sources, because some tests read their own source (`include_str!` in
# `theme.rs`'s icon catalogue test and `goldens.rs`' arm scan) and split it on
# `"\n}\n"`-shaped markers. GitHub's Windows runners check out with
# `core.autocrlf=true`, which gave the catalogue test a CRLF `theme.rs` and a
# brace it could not find — the first CI run, 2026-10-03 (§15 D967). The blobs
# are LF already, so this changes no file, only what a checkout writes.
*.rs text eol=lf
